\section{The nugget}{

A squared exponential kernel is near singular, so a nugget on its diagonal
keeps it factorable. \code{frm()} and \code{frm_sample()} read one tape, the field
\verb{sdgp * L z}, with \code{L} the Cholesky factor of the kernel plus \code{1e-12} on the
correlation: a nugget of \code{1e-12 * sdgp^2}. brms adds an absolute \code{1e-12}. At
\code{sdgp} near 1 the two are one number. A relative nugget does not depend on
the units of y. brms's does: at y times 1000 its kernel does not factor at
the estimates (its run reads Rhat 3.77), and at y divided by 1000 its nugget
floors \code{sigma}.

The two nuggets give one model until the noise reaches the nugget, near
\code{sigma = 1e-6 * sdgp}:
\itemize{
\item With one row per position and an estimated \code{sigma}, \code{sigma} can read low.
\item Where positions repeat, or \code{sigma} is known through \code{se()}, or the family
has no \code{sigma}, \code{sdgp} and the length scale can read low.
}
}

\section{The warning}{

\code{frm()} warns with class \code{frmtmb_gp_nugget}, on top of \code{frmtmb_warning}, when
the 90th percentile over the positions of the nugget's variance over the
noise at a position reaches \code{sqrt(2 / m)} for \code{m} positions, which is about
one standard error of \code{sigma}. The noise at a position is read from the
likelihood's curvature, so the rule is the same for every family and route.
\code{frm()} does not check an \verb{importance =} or \verb{integrate =} fit.
\code{frm_sample(fit)} on a fit with this warning starts at random, because chains
started at that mode stay pinned.
}

\section{What to do about it}{

\code{frm_sample(laplace = TRUE)} integrates the field out, and it mixes fast at
low noise, for the outer parameters only (\code{sigma}, \code{sdgp}, the length
scales): it refuses \code{posterior_epred()}, \code{posterior_linpred()},
\code{posterior_predict()}, \code{fitted()}, \code{predict()}, \code{log_lik()}, \code{loo()} and
\code{conditional_effects()}. The drawn route, the default, is the one for the
field and for predictions, and at low noise it needs long chains (at sigma
1e-3 and 15 rows, Rhat on \code{sdgp} read 1.04 to 2.37 on short chains, and 1.03
on \code{sigma} and 1.07 on \code{sdgp} after an hour, and not yet converged).
\code{gp(x, k = )} has no nugget and reaches brms's numbers when \code{k} is large
enough for the field. Too small a \code{k} is a different failure, with no
warning and with \code{convergence} 0: \code{sin(8 * x)} on 0 to 6 with noise 1e-3 at
\code{k = 30} has a basis that stops
at frequency 6.5, so it reads \code{sigma} 720 times high; \code{k = 60} reads it.
Compare several \code{k}. A smooth trend written as fixed effects also lowers what
the field has to carry.
}

\section{The order of the field}{

\code{frm_sample()}'s \verb{zgp_<term>[i]} columns (brms's name, \code{zgp_gpx[1]}) and
\code{fit$estimates$b} of an exact term hold z, in a farthest-first order of the
positions. brms's own \code{zgp} is z in the order the positions first appear,
through a Cholesky factor in that order, so the two are not equal element by
element. \code{\link[=ranef]{ranef()}}, \code{\link[=coef]{coef()}} and
\code{getME(fit, "b")} read the field, at the sorted distinct positions, where
brms keeps first appearance. None of these is in the order of the rows of
the data. Read a field at the data through \code{posterior_epred()} or
\code{frm_linpred()}, which are right row by row.
}

\examples{
set.seed(1)
d <- data.frame(x = seq(0, 6, length.out = 25))
d$y <- sin(d$x) + rnorm(25, 0, 0.3)
fit <- frm(bf(y ~ gp(x)), data = d)
summary(fit)

# the field at the rows of the data, in their order
